Subject: ntdll: emulate x86 execution breakpoints under Rosetta, with fast64
Date: 2026-10-11
Applies to: CrossOver 26.3.0 Wine sources (crossover-sources-26.3.0.tar.gz), after 0001
Files changed: dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
Origin: ported by hand from wine-rosetta-fix (https://github.com/masonmcbride/wine-rosetta-fix,
LGPL-2.1); fast64 and WINE_ROSETTA_STATS added in this project.
Enabled by: WINE_ROSETTA_EXEC_BREAKPOINTS=1 (plus WINE_ROSETTA_WOW64_BREAKPOINTS=1,
WINE_ROSETTA_CALL_WORKAROUND=1 for 32-bit programs)

Rosetta does not deliver x86 hardware breakpoints. Plutonium arms some, and
BO2 Zombies hung at "Loading fastfile code_pre_gfx_zm" without them. A
thread with armed execution breakpoints is single-stepped until it reaches an
armed address, where the exception is raised as on Windows. fast64: stepping
pauses while a 32-bit thread is inside Wine's 64-bit system-call code, which
cannot hold a 32-bit breakpoint and took about 80% of the steps
(WINE_ROSETTA_FAST64=0 turns it off).
WINE_ROSETTA_STATS=1 logs steps and hits per second, =2 every arm and hit.

Review fixes (2026-10-24), part 1: arming and disarming.
- Debug registers another thread set while this one was suspended (in a
  syscall or in user code) never reached it: under Rosetta wineserver keeps
  them (0001) and does not send them back with the resumed context. The
  SIGUSR1 handler now fetches them from the server after a suspension,
  without ever waiting (a waiting fetch deadlocked when another suspension
  was already pending).
- A WOW64 thread suspended inside a syscall is now armed or disarmed from
  them (rosetta_update_wow64_step, shared with set_thread_wow64_context).
  Before, a breakpoint set on a thread blocked in Sleep never fired, and a
  thread disarmed while waiting kept single-stepping forever.
- Arming clears a stop left over from an earlier disarm (stopping_step),
  which swallowed the first trap of the new breakpoints.
- trap_handler stops stepping (also in the 32-bit frame) when no breakpoint
  is armed any more.
Review fixes (2026-10-24), part 2:
- fast64 stops right after the 64-bit pushfq that saves the 32-bit flags
  (the point the 16-step count approximated, with 3 instructions to spare),
  and the count restarts whenever stepping is armed (a leftover count could
  drop TF for good).
- The application does not see the emulation's TF: pushf stores flags
  without it, a popf that clears TF does not end the emulation, a popf that
  sets TF raises the application's own single-step (DR6.BS), and another
  thread reading a WOW64 thread suspended in a syscall gets EFLAGS without
  it.
- 64-bit processes keep Wine's single-step handling at system calls
  instead of stepping through Wine's unix code (3.4 ms per call armed,
  0.3 ms now).
- The resume-address check (rosetta_initial_breakpoint) no longer runs from
  the SIGUSR1 handler or the user APC/exception dispatchers.
- A breakpoint hit when resuming from a user-mode suspension copies only the
  CONTEXT (CONTEXT_EX's XState offset is relative to the original).
Limits: execution breakpoints only (DR7 LEN 0); the CALL workaround covers
E8 calls only; REP string instructions step as Rosetta delivers them;
resuming exactly at an armed address straight out of a system call or at
thread start runs the first instruction before the breakpoint fires; the
application's own TF on a thread with nothing armed is not delivered
(Rosetta, also without this patch).
Test: tests/src/breakpoint-test.c (13 cases), tests/src/breakpoint-test64.c.
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, like the Wine file it modifies.
